  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/c/cd/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_46.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "We're introduced to Anton Vanko, Red Russia's greatest expert on electricity, who shows off his new red suit for the nation's leader, Khrushchev.&nbsp; It has the ability to disrupt \"every electronic contraption Iron Man possesses!\"&nbsp; They want to take out Iron Man because he is always protecting Tony Stark, whom they want to kill to weaken the US defense efforts.\n\nSoon, Crimson Dynamo is near Stark headquarters and disrupts a test rocket, causing it to plummet toward Earth, and Iron Man rushes up to save it.&nbsp; CD sees that his leader was correct - Iron Man seems always to be around to protect Stark's inventions.&nbsp; Soon, he's attacking Stark's plants all across the country.&nbsp; After the 10<sup>th</sup> one, Stark realizes he's being targeted.&nbsp; There's now even talk in the government that Stark is sabotaging his own companies to help the Reds!\n\nBut Crimson Dynamo, even though he's successfully ruined Stark's defense efforts (which is what the Russians <i>actually </i>wanted), really wants to face Iron Man to show that he's superior.&nbsp; Finally, he attacks the location where Stark himself is, and Iron Man inevitably shows up.&nbsp; They face off...\n\nIron Man has guessed by now that he's disrupting nearby electrical impulses and sets up an \"electrical force field.\"&nbsp; Meanwhile, he secretly records Crimson Dynamo's confession that he was behind the sabotage at the various plants, to help prove Stark wasn't responsible.&nbsp; CD did not count on not being able to disrupt Iron Man's suit, and is soon defeated, especially when Iron Man simulated an order from the Communist leader to terminate him upon his return!\n\nCD is enraged that he was to be so quickly thrown under the bus and agrees to defect to the US, and uncover enough undercover funds to pay for all the damage he's caused, and Stark even offers him a job!&nbsp; Khrushchev is furious and vows revenge!",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "<b>FIXME FIXME FIXME</b>\n[BR] I have a problem with antonvanko's identity, either here or in [LINK:1969:IRON_22|IRON #22] - I have him as Titanium Man I, but I think he's actually Crimson Dynamo I\n!!!!!!!!!\n\nDefinitely a step up from the recent offerings for this mag, it still rises only to just above \"reader\" grade (getting an extra half-point for the introduction of a long-standing Marvel character, albeit a minor one).&nbsp; Crimson Dynamo is indeed never shown to be anything more than a man who seems only intent to show that he can top Iron Man, and in the end, doesn't care as much about the plants he destroyed.&nbsp; Iron Man figures that by robbing the Reds of a brilliant scientist, he's more than made up for the losses.\n\nI do wonder that he's able to take the job, given that Stark will have to play the tape with his confession in it to the government to clear himself, but I suppose that nobody actually knows who's inside the suit, but wouldn't it look peculiar for a Russian scientist to suddenly appear on the scene and take over a research unit?\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nAlso, as always the case in this mag in particular, it's nice that Anton Vanko is able to speak perfect English so that he can talk to Iron Man.&nbsp; Best not to overthink it, I suppose.&nbsp; A somewhat entertaining, but ultimately unmemorable outing, save for the fact that we'll obviously be hearing more from the Crimson Dynamo!\n\nSide Note 1:&nbsp; the \"ultra-sensitive\" miniature reel-to-reel tape recorder inside of Iron Man's armor that we see at the top of page 11 is <i>classic</i>, causing me to laugh out loud.&nbsp; Hey, it was the 60s - <i>hahahaha!</i>\n\nSide Note 2:&nbsp; the back-up tale about the poor, down-on-his-luck reporter \"Fenwick Flubb\" was somewhat amusing, with art by Sol Brodsky, which made it look more suitable as a cartoony-type tale, which indeed it mostly was, but of course, with the standard twist ending you've come to expect from magazines like this!",
  "ownership": "As for my own copy, a lower-grade one came to me as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid $16.50, and it sold on ebay in June, 2021 for $99, netting me almost $70.",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}